zations that spend money on election-related communications would face new regulatory constraints on their expenditures."},{"industry":"media-entertainment","confidence":0.65,"rationale":"Limits on campaign spending could affect demand for political advertising across broadcast, digital, and print media platforms."},{"industry":"finance-banking","confidence":0.55,"rationale":"Financial institutions that make political expenditures or operate PACs could face new restrictions on corporate election spending."}],"plainSummary":"This joint resolution proposes a constitutional amendment that would allow Congress and state governments to set limits on how much money candidates and others can raise and spend to influence elections. It would also permit lawmakers to treat corporations and other legal entities differently from individual people, including by banning corporate spending on elections. The amendment would require ratification by the states to take effect.","classificationMethod":"llm","plain":{"whoItTouches":"Anyone who deals with a federal agency.","confidence":"crs-summary"},"topOverlap":{"bioguideId":"F000454","score":0.04685049700852134},"overlapCount":46},"meaning":{"percentile":55.820565471489495,"median":0.010636331736930488,"n":6331,"ordinary":[],"unattributedShare":0.8099971788837242}},{"billId":"119-hjres-122","bioguideId":"G000592","cycle":2026,"score":0.012196351692905612,"matches":[{"industry":"finance-banking","donorAmount":42000,"donorShare":0.022972368758282975,"billConfidence":0.6,"contribution":0.004446264920957995},{"industry":"ideological-single-issue","donorAmount":24000,"donorShare":0.013127067861875986,"billConfidence":0.9,"contribution":0.0038110842179639964},{"industry":"legal","donorAmount":24500,"donorShare":0.013400548442331735,"billConfidence":0.85,"contribution":0.0036743439277361206},{"industry":"media-entertainment","donorAmount":2000,"donorShare":0.0010939223218229988,"billConfidence":0.75,"contribution":0.0002646586262474997}],"method":{"formula":"score = Σ_industries ( donorShare(industry) × billWeight(industry) ), where billWeight = classifierConfidence / Σ classifierConfidence. Range 0–1, read as a weighted share of disclosed itemized money.","donorProfileCycle":2026,"billClassificationMethod":"llm","unclassifiedDonorShare":0.8099971788837242},"bill":{"id":"119-hjres-122","congress":119,"billType":"hjres","billNumber":"122","title":"Proposing an amendment to the Constitution of the United States relating to the authority of Congress and the States to regulate contributions and expenditures intended to affect elections and to enact public financing systems for political campaigns.","introducedDate":"2025-09-17","latestActionDate":"2025-09-17","latestActionText":"Referred to the House Committee on the Judiciary.","policyArea":"Government Operations and Politics","subjects":[],"sponsorBioguideId":"N000191","cosponsorCount":93,"committeeNames":[],"congressDotGovUrl":"https://www.congress.gov/bill/119th-congress/house-joint-resolution/122","industries":[{"industry":"ideological-single-issue","confidence":0.9,"rationale":"The amendment would directly regulate how advocacy organizations raise and spend money to influence elections."},{"industry":"legal","confidence":0.85,"rationale":"The amendment would reshape campaign-finance law and likely generate substantial litigation and compliance work for law firms and lobbying practices."},{"industry":"media-entertainment","confidence":0.75,"rationale":"Regulations on election-related spending could affect advertising revenue and the market for political communications services."},{"industry":"finance-banking","confidence":0.6,"rationale":"New restrictions on corporate election spending and potential public financing systems could affect financial institutions' political engagement and compliance obligations."}],"plainSummary":"This proposed constitutional amendment would allow Congress and state legislatures to set limits on how much money candidates and others can raise and spend to influence elections. It would also permit them to create public financing systems for campaigns and to treat corporations differently from individual people, including by prohibiting corporations from spending money on elections. The amendment would give lawmakers the authority to write laws implementing these changes.","classificationMethod":"llm","plain":{"whoItTouches":"Anyone who deals with a federal agency.","confidence":"crs-summary"},"topOverlap":{"bioguideId":"W000788","score":0.058071053139282874},"overlapCount":93},"meaning":{"percentile":55.425683146422365,"median":0.010636331736930488,"n":6331,"ordinary":[],"unattributedShare":0.8099971788837242}},{"billId":"119-hr-1106","bioguideId":"G000592","cycle":2026,"score":0.003938120358562796,"matches":[{"industry":"education","donorAmount":25500,"donorShare":0.013947509603243235,"billConfidence":0.5,"contribution":0.0023245849338738725},{"industry":"agriculture","donorAmount":7500,"donorShare":0.004102208706836245,"billConfidence":0.55,"contribution":0.0007520715962533117},{"industry":"pharma","donorAmount":3500,"donorShare":0.001914364063190248,"billConfidence":0.75,"contribution":0.000478591015797562},{"industry":"energy-fossil","donorAmount":3500,"donorShare":0.001914364063190248,"billConfidence":0.6,"contribution":0.00038287281263804955}],"method":{"formula":"score = Σ_industries ( donorShare(industry) × billWeight(industry) ), where billWeight = classifierConfidence / Σ classifierConfidence. Range 0–1, read as a weighted share of disclosed itemized money.","donorProfileCycle":2026,"billClassificationMethod":"llm","unclassifiedDonorShare":0.8099971788837242},"bill":{"id":"119-hr-1106","congress":119,"billType":"hr","billNumber":"1106","title":"Scientific Integrity Act","introducedDate":"2025-02-06","latestActionDate":"2025-02-06","latestActionText":"Referred to the House Committee on Science, Space, and Technology.","policyArea":"Science, Technology, Communications","subjects":[],"sponsorBioguideId":"T000469","cosponsorCount":136,"committeeNames":[],"congressDotGovUrl":"https://www.congress.gov/bill/119th-congress/house-bill/1106","industries":[{"industry":"pharma","confidence":0.75,"rationale":"Pharmaceutical and biotech companies conduct and rely on federally funded research subject to these integrity requirements."},{"industry":"energy-fossil","confidence":0.6,"rationale":"Federal research on climate, emissions, and energy impacts this sector's regulatory environment and public discourse."},{"industry":"energy-renewable","confidence":0.6,"rationale":"Federal research on energy technologies and climate directly affects this sector's market and policy landscape."},{"industry":"agriculture","confidence":0.55,"rationale":"Agricultural research conducted or funded by USDA and other agencies would be subject to these integrity standards."},{"industry":"education","confidence":0.5,"rationale":"Universities receive federal research funding and would need to comply with integrity policies in their federally supported work."}],"plainSummary":"This bill would require every federal agency that funds, conducts, or oversees scientific research to create and enforce a policy protecting scientific integrity. These policies must ensure that research conclusions are not influenced by political considerations, prohibit suppression or alteration of findings, and allow researchers to share their work. Each agency would need to appoint a Scientific Integrity Officer, establish a complaint process, provide training, and submit its policy to the White House Office of Science and Technology Policy for approval.","classificationMethod":"llm","plain":{"whoItTouches":"Anyone who uses a phone, the internet, or a connected device.","confidence":"crs-summary"},"topOverlap":{"bioguideId":"M001225","score":0.04131484571444916},"overlapCount":131},"meaning":{"percentile":18.65424103617122,"median":0.010636331736930488,"n":6331,"ordinary":[],"unattributedShare":0.8